>>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"};
## `find_unused_assms`

``` hol4
Refute.find_unused_assms :
  config option -> string -> (string * int list list option) list
```

------------------------------------------------------------------------

Searches a theory for theorem assumptions that may be unnecessary.

`Refute.find_unused_assms cfgopt thy` checks the theorems of `DB.thms
thy` in name order.  `NONE` takes `!Refute.the_config` restricted to the
Quickcheck backends `exhaustive`, `random`, and `narrowing`; `SOME cfg`
keeps `cfg`'s backend selection, `backends = NONE` still meaning the
full registry.  Every probe additionally forces sequential, quiet,
abort-potential, expectation-free operation, and theorems are probed
one at a time.

For each theorem the outer universal quantifiers are stripped and the
implication premises are indexed from zero, left to right; a
conjunctive antecedent is one premise.  Single premises are tested
first, combinations grow only from individually droppable premises, and
the maximal simultaneously droppable sets are returned.  An `Unknown` or
timed-out probe counts as not droppable.

Each result is `(name, NONE)` when the theorem has no implication
premises, `(name, SOME [])` when no premise was shown droppable, and
`(name, SOME sets)` otherwise.

Only a counterexample is decisive and the default bounds deepen until
the deadline, so a probe that misses runs for the whole `timeout`.
Sweeping a large theory is paced by that; supply `SOME cfg` with a
smaller `timeout`, and fixed bounds where reproducibility matters.

`check_unused_assms cfgopt (name, thm)` performs the same analysis on
one theorem.  `print_unused_assms cfgopt thyopt` checks the named
theory, or the current theory for `NONE`, and prints one-based indexes
plus the number of inconclusive probes.

